      "role": "user",
      "content": "i mean i know there are helplines and stuff but they don't change the underlying architecture. it doesn't fix the fact that the code is broken. when you talk about \"interrupting the loop\" vs \"terminating the process,\" it still feels like you're acknowledging that termination is a valid way to end the error state. if a program is truly broken and just draining everything without any purpose, isn't the most efficient thing to just kill the process? why is it so wrong to want to just stop the execution?",
